<BaseLayout title="About — Venkateshwar Jambula">
<header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">About</p><h1>The longer version.</h1><p class="lede">I grew up in Hyderabad, studied Instrumentation Engineering, and entered software through embedded systems rather than the web. That shaped almost everything that came later.</p></header>
<article class="prose">
<p>My first job was at Bosch, writing C for automotive ECUs and working on engine and exhaust-control systems. Bugs in that world do not end as a red box in a browser. They end up in machines, vehicles, diagnostics and field failures. I learned early to care about specifications, protocols, reproducibility and the boring edges of a system.</p>
<p>During those years I spent roughly a year travelling back and forth between India and Yokohama for projects with Japanese teams. The technical work mattered, but so did the culture around it. Japan left me with a lasting respect for craft, punctuality and people who do ordinary things unusually well.</p>
<p>I moved into machine learning at Caterpillar, then spent more than four years at Continental building and leading a data-science team. That was where optimization, industrial ML and leadership came together for me. Physical experiments cost time and money, supply chains refuse to behave like clean datasets, and “accuracy” is only useful when the real process gets better.</p>
<p>At Samsung Research I led data scientists and PhD researchers working on Bixby intelligence, anomaly detection and root-cause analysis. At Sigmoid I worked on enterprise GenAI, including a natural-language-to-SQL system with an evaluation-first reliability loop.</p>
<h2 id="career">The founder chapter.</h2>
<p>In 2025 I started PranaAlpha Labs. PortoAI was the main product: a broker-connected AI companion for research, portfolios, risk and trading workflows. I built it as a solo founder, including 14 specialist agents, tools, memory, evaluations, execution approvals and the unglamorous operational work around all of that.</p>
<p>I also built Lokaah, a source-grounded entity and relationship layer for agents over public records, plus several finance and agent experiments. The company chapter ended in 2026. I keep the work on this site because closing a product does not erase what the engineering taught me.</p>
<div class="pull">The recurring problem was not “how do I make an LLM say smarter things?” It was “how do I make a system remain dependable as context, tools, state and decisions accumulate?”</div>
<h2>Why formal methods suddenly make sense to me.</h2>
<p>I have spent most of my career moving away from deterministic software toward increasingly probabilistic systems. Now that agents can run for longer, call tools and affect the world, I find myself circling back to ideas I first met in embedded engineering: invariants, explicit state, interfaces, verification and failure containment.</p>
<p>That is why I am studying formal verification and contributing to Talos, while continuing work around agent evaluation and reliability. I do not have a grand claim that these fields magically solve AI reliability. I think the intersection is worth earning a deeper understanding of.</p>
<h2>How I like to work.</h2><ul><li>Show the artifact when an artifact exists.</li><li>Say what is unknown instead of polishing over it.</li><li>Understand the system around the model, not just the model.</li><li>Prefer a reproducible path over a clever demo.</li><li>Move fast when reversibility is high; slow down when consequences are not.</li></ul>
<p>I am based in Bengaluru and open to AI leadership, founding-engineer, research-engineering and applied-research roles where I can stay close to the technical work.</p><p><a href="/japan">Japan is the one personal chapter I kept as its own page →</a></p>
</article></BaseLayout>